I. Introduction
ccurate modeling of coolant flow in regeneratively cooled rocket engines is an essential step in design of cooling circuits. The coolant flow and heat transfer in these engines are influenced by the cooling channel configuration as well as hot-gas-side heating pattern and wall material. Hence, accurate modeling of cooling channel flow and heat transfer require conjugating thermal phenomena among three components of these engines: hot-gas-side combustion and convection, wall conduction, and coolant flow and heat transfer. Three-dimensional integrated numerical model, which combines CFD for the hot-gas combustion environment, coolant flow thermal analysis and finite difference model for wall conduction have been used by Wang and Luong1 to perform a comprehensive thermal analysis of a regeneratively cooled rocket engine. Such CFD models however, are computationally intensive and time consuming.
