A more computationally efficient approach, which is also integrates the three modes of heat transfer, combines two commonly used liquid rocket engine software: RTE and TDK2. In the RTE3 the coolant flow is treated as a one-dimensional flow and the heat transfer from the wall to the coolant is evaluated via Nusselt number correlations for various commonly used coolants. This results in quick convergence of the code making it suitable for cooling circuit design and determining dimensions of cooling channels.
Cooling channels in regenerative cooled liquid rocket engines, as shown in Figure 1, go through curvatures that result in changes (enhancement or diminution) of heat transfer from coolant flow to the walls of cooling channels. Recently, it has been shown that the heat transfer varies differently on various surfaces of cooling channel as the coolant flow enters and exits a curved region4,5,6. The previous work did not include the effect of aspect ratio and treated the developing and depleting secondary flow separately. Similar to the previous work in the present analysis a U-shaped rectangular channel shown in Figure 2 is considered. As the coolant flow enters the straight section of the cooling channel it develops to a fully developed straight channel (the straight channel is long enough to make the flow fully developed). Then the coolant enters the curved section, which results in developing the secondary flow and enhancement in the secondary flow. The fully developed secondary flow is accomplished at some point along the curved section of the cooling channel. As the coolant flow with the secondary flow enters the straight section at the other side of the U-shaped cooling channel the secondary flow diminishes resulting in some changes in the heat transfer coefficient.
Figure 1: Configuration of a cooling channel
Previous studies demonstrated that, as the flow enters the curved region of the cooling channel, the secondary flow and its resulting changes in heat transfer develop gradually along the curved section of the cooling channel. At some point, if the curved region is long enough, the secondary flow becomes fully developed and no further change in heat transfer occurs. It was also demonstrated that the effect of secondary flow on the heat transfer coefficient varies with the position of the wall. Indeed, for the outer wall, there is a significant increase of the heat transfer coefficient while it moderately increases at the sidewalls and even decreases at the inner wall, as show in Figure 3. The exact opposite effects were later noticed for the diminishing secondary flow as the coolant re-enters a straight part of the cooling channel (see Figure 4). Primary correlations were then developed based on those observations but they did not account for the aspect ratio of the channel.
The purpose of this work is to develop a set of comprehensive correlations for heat transfer coefficients as functions of dimensionless radius of curvature, dimensionless distance from the entrance of curvature and the cooling channel aspect ratio. Both developing and diminishing secondary flow will be considered to establish correlations for each wall of the cooling channel: inner wall, outer wall and sidewall. The present work uses a CFD model to establish these correlations.


