II. Modeling of the Curved Channel Flow
In order to analyze the curvature effect on the coolant flow heat transfer a U-shaped rectangular channel, shown in Figure 2, is used. A large number of CFD runs for various radii of curvature and aspect ratios are performed to evaluate the heat transfer coefficients (Nusselt numbers) at different surfaces along the U-shaped tube. The radii of curvature chosen are 100, 150 and 200mm and the aspect ratios are 1, 2, 3 and 3.57 (SSME-like).
Figure 2: Straight and curved channel model used for correlation development
The algorithms of FLUENT software7, which are based on a finite volume method, are used for the CFD modeling in this study. Two solvers can be chosen in FLUENT: a pressure-based solver and a density-based one. The latter is more commonly used to solve high speed and compressible flow fields. The velocities in the cooling channel are expected to vary between 40 m/s and 400 m/s, the fluid was assumed incompressible and the pressure-based solver was used. The incompressible flow was used to eliminate the
