The largest aspect ratio of the channel is based on the SSME cooling channel at the throat area: with the width of W=0.889mm and the height of H=3.175mm. Those dimensions correspond to the aspect ratio of 3.57. To obtain the other aspect ratios, the height is maintained at 3.175 but the width was varied to obtain aspect ratios of 1, 2 and 3. The straight sections of the channel, before and after the curved section, are 205mm long. The U-shaped channel is divided into several cross-sections in order to retrieve the heat transfer coefficient values along all sections. The sections are placed every 10mm along the straight sections of the channel. In the curved part, the sections are placed every 5 degrees. The numerical model takes advantage of the symmetry of the coolant flow and the grid has 30 ! 40 ! 300 nodes (in the halfwidth, height and axial direction, respectively). The mesh is more refined near the walls to obtain better accuracy where the velocity and temperature gradients are largest.
The boundary conditions used for this analysis are consistent with the previously published data for the SSME4 and are as follows:
• The inlet velocity is defined at 39.93 m/s corresponding to a typical coolant velocity of the SSME.
• The inlet coolant temperature is set to 52.4K.
• To the pressure outlet boundary condition was applied a gauge pressure value of 25.93MPa.
• The wall temperature was set to 500K to isolate the effect of curvature on the flow.
In order to analyze only the effect of curvature and eliminate any variation of heat transfer due to the change of coolant properties, it was decided to use constant properties of liquid hydrogen close to its critical point. The properties of Liquid hydrogen used with this model are listed in Table 1.


