III. Results and discussion
As stated earlier, the main objective of this work is to develop improved correlation to account for the effect of both developing and diminishing secondary flow on the heat transfer coefficient. An investigation was first conducted in order to confirm the observations previously made4. Figure 5 represents a plot of contours of z-velocity of the cooling channel in the middle of the curved section of U-shaped channel, obtained using FLUENT software7. A significant variation of velocity is observed with opposite directions on top and bottom of the channel. These results confirm the presence of the secondary flow, which forms two counter rotating secondary flow loops (using the symmetry).
Figure 6 displays contours lines of z-velocity component of the channel flow, computed by FLUENT software, as it exits the curved section and enters the straight section. The results show gradual diminishing secondary flow after the end of the curved section.
Figure 7 shows the variation of heat transfer coefficient along the curved channel with a 50mm radius of curvature and an aspect ratio of 2. The curvature of the channel starts at x=0.205m and it ends at x=0.372m. This figure validates the observations made in the previous studies5. As the flow is entering the curved section of the channel, the heat transfer coefficient increases significantly for the outer wall while it increases by a small amount for the side wall and decreases for the inner wall. Conversely, when re


