V. Concluding Remarks
Correlations for heat transfer coefficients on three surfaces (outer, inner and side) of rectangular cooling channels as the coolant enters and leaves curved section of cooling channels are developed. It is shown that the heat transfer coefficients (Nusselt numbers) vary differently during transition from straight channel to the curved channel and curved channel to the straight one.
The ratio of Nusselt numbers for the transitional flow over fully developed one, in these correlations, are functions of dimensionless radius of curvature, distance from entrance or exit of curved section, and the cooling channel aspect ratio. Two factors influence variation of heat transfer coefficient and Nusselt number: the centrifugal force (pressure) and development of secondary flow. The effects of centrifugal pressure on heat transfer coefficient are mostly present on the outer and inner surfaces of channel. As the coolant enters the curved section due to the increase in the pressure on the outer surface the heat transfer coefficient enhances, while the opposite happens on the inners surface (i.e., the heat transfer coefficient decreases due to the reduction in the pressure forces on the inner surface). As the coolant exits the curved section this process reverses, the heat transfer coefficient on the outer surface lowers while on the inner surface heat transfer coefficient increases.


