The effect of aspect ratio on the heat transfer coefficient is more noticeable at the sidewall. For the inner and outer walls the exponent of the aspect ratio (! ) are relatively small positive numbers – approximately
0.2 for exits of curved section and 0.3 for entrance to the curved section. This positive exponent is an indicative of the fact that as aspect ratio increases it results in an increase in the radial mass depth for coolant flow and larger variation of radial pressure forces. For the sidewall, however, the exponent of aspect ratio is a relatively large negative number – approximately -0.7 for the developing and -0.4 for diminishing cases. This negative exponent results in lowering the effect of aspect ratio increase on the heat transfer coefficient. The increase in the aspect ratio results in larger surface frictional forces and slowing the secondary flow and consequently, lower heat transfer coefficient on the sidewall.
